  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}]$, then ${R}^{4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
